PLACES=[
N('masonic-temple-spokane','Masonic Temple','Spokane','wa','civic','standing',47.65880,-117.42825,'exact','view','walk','','1905–1925',
  'A Neoclassical Masonic hall at 1108 West Riverside Avenue, built for $150,000 and in use from 1905; President Theodore Roosevelt, himself a Mason, turned the first dirt at the groundbreaking. In 1924 and 1925 the lodge, grown large, stretched the front from five bays to nineteen and added an attic storey. It was sold in 2013 and is now an event hall.',
  'A Corinthian colonnade 222 feet long, the only curved front on this stretch of Riverside, with a balustrade above it. It stands three storeys on Riverside and five at the back on Main Avenue, in reinforced concrete. Inside, ancient Egyptian motifs, a ballroom with a sprung floor, the Blue Room with its first red floral carpet, a reopened skylight over an oak stair and a 500-seat auditorium with a balcony; when the hall can be visited was not checked.',
  'Nineteen columns on a curving street, half of them added in 1925.',
  'A private event hall; look from Riverside Avenue. '+DT,
  [RAHD],
  brick='?'),
N('elks-temple-spokane','Elks Temple','Spokane','wa','civic','standing',47.65862,-117.42881,'exact','view','walk','','1921',
  'The Spokane lodge of the Elks built this Renaissance Revival temple at 1116 West Riverside Avenue in 1921 for $200,000, when the lodge had nearly 8,000 members, second only to Los Angeles among Elks lodges. The club later moved out to a smaller hall in Spokane Valley. North Coast Life Insurance bought the building in 1981 and restored it in 1983, winning an award from the Washington Trust for Historic Preservation.',
  'Smooth sandstone over reinforced concrete, an Ionic colonnade with balustrades along the second storey, a tile frieze along the top, and arched windows four to each side of a recessed central door. Who designed it is not settled: the design is put down to Edward J. Baume, and Kirtland Cutter\'s name has also been tied to it. Interior not checked.',
  'The third of three colonnades in a row on Riverside.',
  'Offices; look from Riverside Avenue. '+DT,
  [RAHD],
  brick='no'),
N('spokane-club-building','Spokane Club','Spokane','wa','civic','standing',47.65892,-117.42723,'exact','view','walk','','1910–1911',
  'The clubhouse of the Spokane Club, a members-only club, at 1002 West Riverside Avenue. It was built in 1910 for $425,000 in the Georgian Revival style to the design of Cutter and Malmgren, and opened in 1911. It is still the club\'s home, used for dining, rooms and sport. The club\'s earlier building of 1901, by John K. Dow, stands at Washington Street and Riverside and is listed as the Spokane Club Building-Legion Building.',
  'A building 150 feet long on Riverside: the ground storey almost all terra cotta, the three above mostly red brick with white terra-cotta trim, and an entrance bay standing forward in the middle. Interior not checked; it is for members.',
  'Kirtland Cutter\'s firm at the head of Riverside\'s civic row.',
  'A private club; look from Riverside Avenue. '+DT,
  [RAHD,W('National Register of Historic Places listings in Spokane County, Washington')],
  brick='yes',bcol='red',arch='Cutter and Malmgren',archw='Wikipedia, Riverside Avenue Historic District: "designed by Cutter and Malmgren"'),
N('cathedral-our-lady-of-lourdes-spokane','Cathedral of Our Lady of Lourdes','Spokane','wa','worship','standing',47.65802,-117.42878,'exact','view','walk','','1902–1908',
  'The Catholic cathedral of Spokane, at 1115 West Riverside Avenue. The parish goes back to a first Mass of 1881 in a carpenter\'s shop; the present church was begun in 1902, held services in its finished basement from 1903, was completed in 1907 and dedicated in 1908, and became a cathedral when the Diocese of Spokane was made in 1913. It was designed in the Romanesque Revival style by Preusse and Zittel, with changes by Julian and Williams.',
  'Two bell towers 125 feet high, three round-arched doors reached up a flight of steps (the first doors were replaced by bronze ones), a large wheel window over the centre, and a brick veneer with white terra-cotta trim over reinforced concrete and masonry. Inside are ribbed barrel vaults and Bavarian stained glass, restored in 1991 and again in 2023; the sanctuary was redone in marble in 2019. Mass times could not be read from the parish page when this was written.',
  'Twin towers that stand above the rest of Riverside.',
  'An active cathedral of the Diocese of Spokane; the church itself is the place to ask about visiting. '+DT,
  [W('Cathedral of Our Lady of Lourdes (Spokane, Washington)'),RAHD,L('The cathedral\'s own site','https://spokanecathedral.com/')],
  brick='yes',bcol='red',arch='Preusse and Zittel; Julian and Williams',archw='Wikipedia, Riverside Avenue Historic District: designed by "local firm Preusse and Zittel with modifications by another local firm, Julian and Williams"',hours='?'),
N('st-aloysius-church-spokane','St. Aloysius Church','Spokane','wa','worship','standing',47.66810,-117.40506,'exact','view','walk','','1909–1911',
  'The Jesuit parish church on the grounds of Gonzaga University, at 330 East Boone Avenue. The Jesuits\' first church here, St. Joseph\'s, was a plain wooden building; it was moved off the site, the foundation stone of St. Aloysius was set in October 1909, and the new church opened on October 12, 1911, the day after the last Mass at St. Joseph\'s. It was designed in the Romanesque style by Preusse and Zittel. The parish was formed in 1890, and Bing Crosby, who grew up nearby, was an altar boy and sang in the choir.',
  'Round-arched Romanesque work, and stained-glass windows round the church showing Jesuit saints and St. Aloysius. Mass times could not be read from the parish page when this was written; interior not checked.',
  'Gonzaga\'s Jesuit church, where Bing Crosby served at the altar.',
  'An active parish church on the Gonzaga University campus; look from East Boone Avenue. Parking on the streets round the campus (not checked).',
  [W('St. Aloysius Church (Spokane, Washington)'),L('The parish\'s own site','https://stalschurch.org/')],
  brick='?',arch='Preusse and Zittel',archw='Wikipedia, St. Aloysius Church: "designed in Romanesque style by the Spokane firm of Preusse & Zittel"',hours='?'),
N('empire-state-building-spokane','Empire State Building','Spokane','wa','civic','standing',47.65762,-117.42406,'exact','view','walk','','1899–1900',
  'An office building at the corner of Riverside Avenue and Lincoln Street, across from the federal building, designed by John K. Dow and built for Charles Sweeny, a Civil War veteran who made a fortune in the Coeur d\'Alene mines of Idaho. His Empire State-Idaho Company of 1900 gave the building its name. Its building ran over cost because of a steel shortage and the brick shortage that followed the fire of 1889. It was renamed the Great Western Savings Building in 1961.',
  'A steel frame faced in buff brick with terra-cotta panels and trim; tall piers rise through the third to fifth storeys with arcades of windows between them on the Riverside and Lincoln fronts. Interior not checked.',
  'A mining fortune\'s office block, in buff brick, after the fire.',
  'An office building; look from Riverside Avenue and Lincoln Street. '+DT,
  [W('Empire State Building (Spokane, Washington)')],
  brick='no',bcol='buff',arch='John K. Dow',archw='Wikipedia, Empire State Building (Spokane): "designed by architect John K. Dow"'),
N('review-building-spokane','Review Building','Spokane','wa','civic','standing',47.65764,-117.42609,'exact','view','walk','','1891',
  'The six-storey home of the Spokane Falls Review, later The Spokesman-Review, on the southeast corner of Riverside Avenue and Monroe Street. It was built in 1891, two years after the fire, in the Romanesque Revival style to the design of Chauncey B. Seaton, and at 146 feet was the city\'s first high-rise and its tallest building for about ten years.',
  'A Romanesque Revival block, built with terra cotta, at the corner of Riverside and Monroe. Interior not checked.',
  'Spokane\'s first high-rise, put up two years after the fire.',
  'Look from Riverside Avenue and Monroe Street. '+DT,
  [W('Review Building'),W('List of tallest buildings in Spokane')],
  brick='?',arch='Chauncey B. Seaton',archw='Wikipedia, Review Building (infobox): architect Chauncey B. Seaton'),
N('cathedral-st-john-evangelist-spokane','Cathedral of St. John the Evangelist','Spokane','wa','worship','standing',47.64496,-117.41039,'exact','open','walk','','1925–1954',
  'The cathedral of the Episcopal Diocese of Spokane, on Grand Boulevard on the South Hill, in the Rockwood neighbourhood. Three parishes merged to form it in 1929; building had begun in 1925 under the architect Harold C. Whitehouse of Whitehouse and Price, who toured Europe and chose English Gothic with some French touches. The first part was ready for services late in 1929; work began again in 1948, and the chancel, transepts and Gothic tower were finished by 1954.',
  'Solid masonry: sandstone from near Tacoma outside, Idaho sandstone inside and some Indiana limestone. Carvings and Gothic stained glass by the Charles J. Connick studio and Willet Hauser, and an Aeolian-Skinner organ of 1957. The cathedral welcomes visitors for self-guided visits on weekdays and gives guided tours on Fridays; its page asks visitors to use the Welcome Center door on 12th Avenue.',
  'English Gothic, built across thirty years on the South Hill.',
  'An active cathedral that welcomes visitors (its own page); use the Welcome Center entrance on 12th Avenue. Street parking round the cathedral (not checked).',
  [W('Cathedral of St. John the Evangelist (Spokane, Washington)'),L('The cathedral\'s own page','https://www.stjohns-cathedral.org/')],
  brick='no',arch='Whitehouse and Price',archw='Wikipedia: "founding architect Harold C. Whitehouse (of the Spokane firm of Whitehouse & Price)"',
  hours='Visits Monday to Friday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.; guided tours Friday noon to 3 p.m.; services Saturday 5:30 p.m., Sunday 8 and 10:30 a.m., Wednesday noon'),
N('spokane-county-courthouse','Spokane County Courthouse','Spokane','wa','civic','standing',47.66472,-117.42861,'exact','view','walk','','1893–1895',
  'The county courthouse on the north bank of the river, in the West Central neighbourhood, on land given about ten years earlier by the settler David P. Jenkins. Ground was broken in 1893, partly to help the young city through the Panic of that year, and the building was finished by November 1895. The 29-year-old Willis A. Ritchie won the design contest; Kirtland Cutter came second. It still holds county offices and the Superior Court.',
  'French Renaissance and Chateauesque: an H-shaped plan with conical towers on the corners of each arm topped by spires, a central tower nearly nine storeys high, and steep Mansard roofs of slate. Its load-bearing walls are dry-pressed brick, among the first in Spokane made locally (by the Washington Brick and Lime Company) and not shipped from the east. Interior not checked.',
  'A chateau for a county seat, with its tower over the river.',
  'A working county courthouse; look from the streets of the county campus. Hours not checked. Street parking round the campus (owners and fees not checked).',
  [W('Spokane County Courthouse')],
  brick='?',arch='Willis A. Ritchie',archw='Wikipedia, Spokane County Courthouse: "A design contest prior to construction was won by architect Willis Ritchie"'),
N('monroe-street-bridge-spokane','Monroe Street Bridge','Spokane','wa','landmark','standing',47.66050,-117.42670,'exact','public','walk','','1911',
  'A concrete deck arch over the Spokane River just west of the falls, opened on November 23, 1911 and built by the city to the design of its engineer John Chester Ralston, with ornament by Cutter and Malmgren. On completion it was the largest concrete arch in the United States. It is the third bridge here: a wooden one of 1889 burned in 1890, and a steel one of 1892 was judged unsafe in 1905. Closed in January 2003, it was taken down to its central arch, rebuilt to match and reopened on September 17, 2005.',
  'The great central arch over the gorge, seen from the bridge\'s own walkways and from the river banks and Riverfront Park.',
  'The arch that sits right beside the Spokane Falls.',
  'A street bridge built and owned by the City of Spokane, with walkways on both sides. '+DT,
  [W('Monroe Street Bridge (Spokane, Washington)')],
  brick='no',arch='John Chester Ralston; Cutter and Malmgren',archw='Wikipedia, Monroe Street Bridge: "designed by city engineer John Chester Ralston ... with ornamentation provided by ... Cutter & Malmgren"'),
N('davenport-hotel-spokane','The Davenport Hotel','Spokane','wa','hotel','standing',47.65681,-117.42435,'exact','open','walk','','1913–1914',
  'A 406-room hotel at 10 South Post Street, named after Louis Davenport, who began with a restaurant on this corner; Kirtland Cutter had already joined two buildings into one for him in 1904. The hotel tower went up in eight months in 1913, using horse carts, steam jacks and hand tools, and opened in September 1914, designed by Cutter in the Renaissance and Spanish Revival styles. Davenport sold it in 1945; after many owners it became a motel in the early 1960s and was later foreclosed on; Walt and Karen Worthy bought and restored it, reopening it on July 15, 2002.',
  'A working hotel. Its lobby, called "Spokane\'s living room", was praised in 1921 as among the finest in America; the hotel opened with a pipe organ, air conditioning and a central vacuum system. When the public rooms may be seen by people who are not guests was not checked.',
  'Kirtland Cutter\'s grand hotel, lobby and all.',
  'A working hotel (its own site). '+DT,
  [W('The Davenport Hotel (Spokane, Washington)'),L('The hotel\'s own site','https://www.thedavenporthotel.com/')],
  brick='?',arch='Kirtland Cutter',archw='Wikipedia, The Davenport Hotel: "designed by architect Kirtland Cutter"',hours='?'),
N('federal-building-post-office-spokane','U.S. Post Office, Courthouse, and Custom House','Spokane','wa','civic','standing',47.65820,-117.42540,'exact','view','walk','','1908–1909',
  'The first large federal building of the Inland Empire, on Riverside Avenue at Lincoln Street, on a site bought in 1903. James Knox Taylor, Supervising Architect of the Treasury, designed it in 1906 and 1907; it was built in 1908 and 1909 and opened in October 1909, after a visit from President Taft. Louis A. Simon designed the addition of 1941 to match. It still serves as a courthouse for the federal court of the Eastern District of Washington.',
  'A symmetrical classical front on a granite-faced basement. Inside, marble pilasters, floors and wainscot, plaster panels, coffered ceilings and terrazzo; the third-floor courtroom, restored in 1994, keeps its first oak rails, benches and desks. A federal courthouse with security at the door; interior visiting not checked.',
  'A Treasury classic of 1909, with a matching wing of 1941.',
  'A federal courthouse; look from Riverside Avenue and Lincoln Street. '+DT,
  [W('United States Post Office, Courthouse, and Custom House (Spokane, Washington)')],
  brick='?',arch='James Knox Taylor; Louis A. Simon',archw='Wikipedia: "James Knox Taylor, the Supervising Architect of the U.S. Treasury Department, designed the building"; "The expansion was designed by Louis A. Simon"'),
N('holy-names-academy-building-spokane','Holy Names Academy Building','Spokane','wa','civic','standing',47.66900,-117.39236,'exact','view','walk','','1891–1975',
  'The Sisters of the Holy Names\' academy, at 1216 North Superior Street by the Spokane River. The central block was built in 1891 to the design of Patrick Donohoe; in 1903 Preusse and Zittel added the north and south wings and the centre was altered to match, losing its entrance portico and bell towers. The last high school class graduated in 1975. When it was listed in 1986 it was being made into apartments, which it still is.',
  'A four-storey red brick building in a U-shaped plan on a foundation of cut granite, a central block with a Mansard roof between two gabled wings. Private apartments; interior not open to the public.',
  'A convent school of 1891, now flats.',
  'Private apartments; look from North Superior Street. Street parking nearby (not checked).',
  [L('National Register record (86000959)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/86000959'),L('National Register nomination form (1986)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/GetAsset/9001a27b-baa8-4aec-af9e-23a312235fa6')],
  brick='yes',bcol='red',arch='Patrick Donohoe; Preusse and Zittel',archw='National Register form: the central block "designed by Patrick Donohoe"; the 1903 wings "designed by Preusse and Zittel"'),
N('great-northern-clocktower-spokane','Great Northern Clock Tower','Spokane','wa','rail','standing',47.66149,-117.41904,'exact','public','walk','','1902',
  'The tower of the Great Northern Railway\'s depot on Havermale Island, which brought trains into the middle of the city from 1902. When the tracks were taken out to make ready for Expo \'74, the depot was pulled down in 1973, but the clock tower was kept after people pressed to save it. It stands in Riverfront Park, owned and run by Spokane Parks and Recreation, and the park\'s logo is drawn from it.',
  'A tall, thin tower with a pointed top and round clock faces, standing alone in the park beside the Clock Tower Meadow. The rest of the depot is gone.',
  'All that is left of a depot that once filled the island.',
  'In Riverfront Park, run by Spokane Parks and Recreation, on Havermale Island; reached on foot through the park. Park hours not checked. '+DT,
  [W('Riverfront Park (Spokane, Washington)')],
  brick='?'),
N('old-national-bank-building-spokane','Old National Bank Building (U.S. Bank Building)','Spokane','wa','civic','standing',47.65833,-117.41917,'exact','view','walk','','1910–1911',
  'The headquarters of the Old National Bank of Spokane, founded in 1891, and the first skyscraper of the Inland Northwest. D. H. Burnham and Company of Chicago won the design competition; it was the last office building Burnham designed. Begun in March 1910, it was finished eight months later and opened in January 1911; at 219 feet it was the tallest building in Washington until 1911 and in Spokane until 1929. U.S. Bank occupies it now.',
  'A Chicago School tower on a fireproofed steel frame faced in white semi-enamel terra cotta, with a four-foot cornice of Vermont granite over the first storey and an arcade along the top floors. The lobby has been altered: a mezzanine put in, the ceiling lowered and the granite Ionic columns stripped. Interior not checked.',
  'Daniel Burnham\'s last office tower, in white terra cotta.',
  'A working bank and offices; look from Riverside Avenue. '+DT,
  [W('U.S. Bank Building (Spokane)'),W('List of tallest buildings in Spokane')],
  brick='no',arch='D. H. Burnham and Company',archw='Wikipedia, U.S. Bank Building (Spokane): "The winning design was awarded to D.H. Burnham & Company of Chicago"'),
N('paulsen-medical-and-dental-building-spokane','Paulsen Medical and Dental Building','Spokane','wa','civic','standing',47.65778,-117.41861,'exact','view','walk','','1928–1929',
  'A 15-storey Art Deco tower with a Spanish Moorish skin, built in 1928 and 1929 to the design of Gustav Pehrson next to the August Paulsen Building of 1908 to 1911; together they are called the Paulsen Center. August Paulsen, a Danish-born businessman, died before it was finished, and his family lived in its penthouse until 2007. At 221 feet it was the tallest building in Spokane from 1929 until 1981.',
  'The tower and its penthouse over Riverside Avenue, across the street from the Old National Bank Building. Interior not checked.',
  'Spokane\'s tallest building for fifty years.',
  'Offices; look from Riverside Avenue. '+DT,
  [W('August Paulsen'),W('List of tallest buildings in Spokane')],
  brick='?',arch='Gustav Albin Pehrson',archw='Wikipedia, August Paulsen: "The Paulsen Medical and Dental Building was designed by Gustav Pehrson"'),
]
